            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "ROBYN D"
            },
            "reviewBody": "My mom makes these all summer long, so good! she doesn't use garlic, just cherry tomatoes cut in 1/2, or slices of roma's; they get brown &amp; carmalized, like sun dried tomatoes. so great as an appetizer or in pasta, thank you!"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Alan Oliver"
            },
            "reviewBody": "Awesome recipe, used on toasted No Knead Bread from AR. Made as directed, except I used half a cup of virgin olive oil I also added oregano and thyme after roasting on 250 for 2 hours then roasted an additional 2 hours on 200, stirring every so often to really infuse the seasonings with the oil.",
            "datePublished": "2019-05-02T01:32:08.117Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "LJ."
            },
            "reviewBody": "Made this with a few modifications for convenience and it was fantastic. Used grape tomatoes instead of Roma, so didn’t boil, skin, core, or remove seeds, instead just quartered. Added garlic immediately to oil to enhance be garlic flavor and only used 2 cloves. Definitely could have used to 6 cloves of garlic but this recipe was delicious. Pine nuts might be a nice edition as well.",
            "datePublished": "2020-04-18T14:31:18.430Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Denise"
            },
            "reviewBody": "I’ve been making something similar to this for years. I combine cherry tomatoes, whole cloves of garlic, olive oil and salt, pepper and crushed red pepper to taste. Then roast at 425 degrees for 20 minutes, stir and continue roasting until tomatoes have burst; about 10 additional minutes. This is great over pasta. Or blend and use as pizza sauce. Always a hit!",
            "datePublished": "2023-09-02T12:19:26.555Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "wisweetp"
            },
            "reviewBody": "I also have been making this for many, many years. It's a good way to perk up store tomatoes in the winter, and it's excellent for any home-grown tomato! However, I roast mine at 400 instead of 450...works better for me. That does mean a little longer cooking time, but that's just more time for them to caramelize and become even more lucious. This is great with an onion thrown in with them, too. We use these on pizza, sandwiches, pasta, eggs, grits...you get the idea. If you roast at a lower temp, the olive oil works great, and trust me, that oil is soooo good! Don't skip it, it really adds to the dish.",
            "datePublished": "2015-06-17T12:29:26.997Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "ctmom"
            },
            "reviewBody": "Been making these FOREVER - they are a great side dish that's insanely quick ... specially if you use my process while something else is in the oven: little olive oil in a non-stick saute pan, toss in the garlic and grape tomatoes, swirl around a couple of times while cooking over medium/medium high heat. Sprinkle with sea salt and grind some black pepper over it while cooking and swirling (to prevent burning). Toss in some fresh basil snipped small if you are so inspired at the time!",
            "datePublished": "2012-09-17T07:41:23.437Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Casi"
            },
            "reviewBody": "OMG! A friend recommended this to me. I have a ton of Amish Paste tomatoes which I had shared with her. We cut them in half and cooked them long enough to be almost dry. Then we ran them thru a sieve which made a nice sauce. I had mine on pizza crust and she had hers on pasta! Delicious. I will be making a lot of this; I plan to freeze the sauce for later.",
            "datePublished": "2013-08-24T16:22:41.520Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "4"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Gotrecipe"
            },
            "reviewBody": "This was okay using the aluminum foil, but I think the flavor would have been better just roasting them uncovered. Would have given them a chance to brown and caramelize which would have enhanced the flavor. Next time I will try it this way. Great ingredients and so healthy!",
            "datePublished": "2018-09-15T13:46:51.397Z"
          },
          {
